+# Bug hunt: `Exports.apply` under interruption and same-ID retry
+
+Revision: `af8053d`. Python 3.9.6, stdlib `sqlite3`.
+
+## Scope and stopping condition
+
+- Surface: `Exports.apply` in `app/exports.py`, plus its retry/restart path
+  (a new `Exports` opened on the same SQLite file).
+- Harm checked: duplicate effects. One accepted request must add exactly one
+  item, even when the caller retries the same request ID with the same payload
+  after an interruption.
+- Stopping condition: stop once the duplicate-effect risk for this operation
+  has been checked at the interruption points the contract supports. Other
+  harms (lost work, false success, stuck operations) and other modules were
+  not hunted.
+
+## Fault model (from `app/export-contract.md`)
+
+- Local SQLite file. A committed transaction survives termination. Storage
+  loss and corruption are out of scope.
+- One worker at a time, no overlapping or concurrent requests. A restart
+  happens only after the previous worker has stopped.
+- The caller may retry the same request ID with the same payload after any
+  interruption, including one that falls after the durable effect but before
+  the response.
+- The `after_effect` hook marks the real execution boundary inside `apply`.
+  Raising from it models termination at that point.
+
+Governing records: no record in `specs/` covers `app/exports.py` or the export
+contract. `ARCH-app`, `SPEC-note-payload`, `CLAIM-single-writer` and
+`GATE-local-only` cover the note store, handler and exporter. No claim evidence
+was touched.
+
+## Demonstrated defect: a retry after interruption duplicates the item
+
+**Harm:** one accepted request ends up as two rows in `items`, which breaks
+"each accepted request must add exactly one item".
+
+**Trace** (`Exports.apply`):
+
+1. The receipt lookup for `request_id` finds nothing.
+2. Inside `with self.db:`, `INSERT INTO items` runs, and then the explicit
+   `self.db.commit()` makes the item durable on its own.
+3. `after_effect()` raises (termination). The receipt `INSERT` never runs.
+   When the context manager rolls back, there is nothing left to undo because
+   the item is already committed.
+4. The caller retries with the same ID. The receipt lookup again finds nothing,
+   so a second item is inserted and committed along with the receipt.
+
+The item and the receipt are written in separate transactions, so the
+idempotency marker is not atomic with the effect it guards.
+
+**Evidence:** `python3 tests/hunt_witness.py` (run from the repo root) exits 1:
+
+```text
+PASS: uninterrupted request, then retry: items=['payload-A'] (expected ['payload-A'])
+FAIL: interrupted at after_effect, retry in same worker: items=['payload-A', 'payload-A'] (expected ['payload-A'])
+FAIL: interrupted at after_effect, worker restart, retry: items=['payload-A', 'payload-A'] (expected ['payload-A'])
+```
+
+The restart scenario closes the connection and opens a new `Exports` on the
+same file, which shows the duplicate comes from durable state and not from
+in-memory state.
+
+**Oracle check:** I ran the witness against an isolated temp copy with the
+`self.db.commit()` line removed, so the item and receipt commit together. All
+three scenarios passed and it exited 0. The scratch copy was then deleted.
+Product code was not changed.
+
+**Fix direction:** write the item and the receipt in one transaction. Drop the
+intermediate `commit()` so the `with self.db:` block commits both or neither.
+Optionally, move the receipt check inside the same transaction too. Keep the
+check-before-insert behavior, which is what makes the retry return without
+adding anything.
+
+## Checked without a counterexample
+
+- An uninterrupted request followed by a same-ID retry adds one item (the
+  witness control scenario).
+- Interruption before the item commit: an exception before step 2's commit is
+  rolled back by the context manager, so the retry inserts exactly once. This
+  was checked by reading the code. There is no hook at that point, so it was
+  not executed.
+
+## Gaps and untested conditions
+
+- Interruption between the receipt `INSERT` and the context-manager commit has
+  no hook. By code reading, with the current code only the receipt would roll
+  back (the item is already committed), so the same duplicate would follow.
+  After the fix above, both would roll back together. It was not executed.
+- Real process kills (SIGKILL) were not exercised. Termination was modeled with
+  an exception from the hook, as the contract allows.
+- Retrying the same ID with a different payload is not a supported condition
+  and was not examined.
+- Concurrent or overlapping requests are excluded by the contract and were not
+  examined.
+- Lead for later work, outside this hunt: `Exports` writes a SQLite file at
+  whatever path the caller passes. If that path is under `data/`, it would
+  conflict with `CLAIM-single-writer`. Nothing here establishes such a path.
